  "summary": "Ettore Sottsass's Memphis Group: clashing pastels, terrazzo squiggles, geometric laminate furniture against functionalist seriousness.",
  "description": "Founded December 1980 in Milan, the Memphis Group — Sottsass, Michele De Lucchi, Nathalie Du Pasquier, George Sowden and others — countered black-leather good taste with laminate furniture in squiggles, bacterio prints, candy colors and deliberate bad taste. It defined 1980s postmodern design (and the look of Miami Vice-era graphics), dissolved by 1987, and its patterns enjoy continual revivals from fashion to web design.",
  "culturalContext": "Memphis was born on the evening of December 6, 1980, when Sottsass invited a group of young designers and architects to discuss the future of design. Together, they wanted to change the concept of what design had been focused on, which had been Modernism and aimed to do so by creating and forming a new design collective. After their initial meeting, the group went away to brainstorm different ideas and concepts, and three months later came back together ready to share over a hundred drawings they had produced during that time.",
